<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP is permitted.

  The host runs on an existing supervisor PC inside the factory LAN. It has no
  domain name and no certificate, so HTTPS is not available to it, and the
  server address is a setting the supervisor types in rather than a fixed host
  this file could name.

  Android matches these rules by hostname, not by address range, so there is
  no way to write "private addresses only" here — a <domain> entry of
  192.168.0.0 matches that one host and nothing else. Scoping it per-IP would
  mean editing the APK whenever the host PC moves, which is exactly the
  coupling the settings screen exists to avoid.

  So this is a real limitation, stated rather than disguised: traffic between
  the phones and the host is unencrypted on the factory LAN. It carries sizes,
  barcodes and label photographs — no personal data, no credentials, since
  there is no password to send (§4.9). Chapter 4 §4.9 records it and §6.4
  lists TLS on the host as further work.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
